Well, not really. The same has been said of AOL, of
Portal, of Compuserve, and of many other Usenet
access means that opened posting to a larger
participating audience.

Truth is, it is the lack of experience of the
audience, not the usability or lack thereof of the
access mechanism, that is the entire problem. The
surge of low quality postings at the start of every
University school year used to be spoken of as the
September problem. Now we have September all year
long, with a more steady influx of posters who have
newly discovered Usenet, year around.

If ease of access were the cause of the problem,
you'd see an improvement in quality when access was
made harder, yet you didn't see some surge of Usenet
quality last year, when AOL dropped Usenet access
for its many millions of customers.

Once upon a time, only grad students, government
employees, and University professors could post to
Usenet, and using what from today look like the most
primitive of tools. That didn't stop low quality
postings then, nor does ease of access cause them
now.

